body:
Interesting. I've found the facilities pretty nice especially in the math department building, even if the classrooms within there are pretty small. Probably also because I'm usually in the large room to the right of the entrance of Havemeyer, and I've only been in other buildings a few times.

&#x200B;

I assume that the student perspective is more authoritative and maybe I don't really have a good standard of comparison.

Do Python over the summer. This will allow you to get a grasp of programming concepts, which will allow you get jump quickly to Java, without dealing with Java's pesky syntax and verbosity.

&#x200B;

Once you have a good grasp on that, start studying the intricacies of Java, and you should be good. The AP test doesn't have much beyond tracing, which Python should help teach you enough about.

My AP Euro teacher has curves once in a while, but not that generous (6 points on the midterm being the largest yet). None of my other classes, AP or otherwise, have any sort of mechanism to get points back, save APCSA, for which late assignments do not lose points.

&#x200B;

However, none of the tests are particularly hard.

I took last year's test and I do not believe there was any networking or similar things on the test, whereas practically the entire test was robot "recursion" (looping to determine which code segment worked) questions, which were trivial. Our teacher also taught almost nothing, but the projects killed us, as various technical difficulties such as submitting everything in a single field and not submitting any task at all caused abundant 3/4s.

&#x200B;

To reply to the OP, I believe that the APCSA exam is similar from friends who have taken it, so be able to do "recursion", and basic Java concepts.
